Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they will be used  

• packaging.id : primary key that identifies every kind of package (bottle, carton, pallet …) – we need it both for hierarchy traversal and for the final name lookup.  
• packaging.name : human‑readable name – this is what we will finally return.  
• packaging_relations.packaging_id : “parent” or container package – helps us find the top‑level containers (those which never appear as a child).  
• packaging_relations.contains_id : “child” that is packed inside its parent – lets us walk down through all nested levels.  
• packaging_relations.qty : how many units of the child are inside the parent – the numbers must be multiplied at each step and summed across alternative paths.  

Typical conditions we will need  

• packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT DISTINCT contains_id …) → select only the top‑level containers.  
• item_id NOT IN (SELECT DISTINCT packaging_id …)    → select leaf items (they do not themselves contain anything).  
• HAVING SUM(total_qty) > 500            → keep only the (container, item) pairs whose accumulated quantity exceeds 500.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Ten exploratory / increasing‑complexity queries
--------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at all package definitions
SELECT id, name
FROM packaging
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect the raw parent‑child relations
SELECT packaging_id, contains_id, qty
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. All distinct containers that ever wrap something
SELECT DISTINCT packaging_id
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. All distinct children that are ever wrapped
SELECT DISTINCT contains_id
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Candidate top‑level containers (they never appear as a child)
WITH children AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT contains_id FROM packaging_relations
)
SELECT p.id, p.name
FROM   packaging AS p
WHERE  p.id IN (SELECT DISTINCT packaging_id FROM packaging_relations)
  AND  p.id NOT IN (SELECT * FROM children)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Leaf (bottom) items that never wrap anything else
WITH parents AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT packaging_id FROM packaging_relations
)
SELECT p.id, p.name
FROM   packaging AS p
WHERE  p.id NOT IN (SELECT * FROM parents)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Show direct children of one example container (say id = 511)
SELECT pr.contains_id, p.name, pr.qty
FROM   packaging_relations AS pr
JOIN   packaging          AS p ON p.id = pr.contains_id
WHERE  pr.packaging_id = 511;
```

```sql
-- 8. Build a recursive list (container, item, cumulative_qty) for all paths
WITH RECURSIVE paths(container_id, item_id, total_qty) AS (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           pr.qty
    FROM   packaging_relations AS pr
  UNION ALL
    SELECT paths.container_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           paths.total_qty * pr.qty
    FROM   paths
    JOIN   packaging_relations AS pr
           ON pr.packaging_id = paths.item_id
)
SELECT *
FROM   paths
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Accumulate total quantity of every (container, item) pair
WITH RECURSIVE paths(container_id, item_id, total_qty) AS (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           pr.qty
    FROM   packaging_relations AS pr
  UNION ALL
    SELECT paths.container_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           paths.total_qty * pr.qty
    FROM   paths
    JOIN   packaging_relations AS pr
           ON pr.packaging_id = paths.item_id
),
totals AS (
  SELECT container_id,
         item_id,
         SUM(total_qty) AS total_qty
  FROM   paths
  GROUP  BY container_id, item_id
)
SELECT *
FROM   totals
ORDER  BY total_qty DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER:
--     Top‑level containers whose quantity of at least one item exceeds 500,
--     showing container name, item name and the accumulated quantity.
WITH top_level AS (                      -- containers that are never children
  SELECT DISTINCT pr.packaging_id AS id
  FROM   packaging_relations pr
  WHERE  pr.packaging_id NOT IN (
         SELECT DISTINCT contains_id FROM packaging_relations
  )
),
RECURSIVE paths(container_id, item_id, total_qty) AS (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           pr.qty
    FROM   packaging_relations AS pr
  UNION ALL
    SELECT paths.container_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           paths.total_qty * pr.qty
    FROM   paths
    JOIN   packaging_relations AS pr
           ON pr.packaging_id = paths.item_id
),
totals AS (
  SELECT container_id,
         item_id,
         SUM(total_qty) AS total_qty
  FROM   paths
  WHERE  container_id IN (SELECT id FROM top_level)
  GROUP  BY container_id, item_id
  HAVING total_qty > 500
)
SELECT pc.name AS container_name,
       pi.name AS item_name,
       totals.total_qty
FROM   totals
JOIN   packaging AS pc ON pc.id = totals.container_id
JOIN   packaging AS pi ON pi.id = totals.item_id
ORDER  BY pc.name, pi.name
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries start with simple inspection of the two relevant tables, gradually build the hierarchical view with a recursive CTE, and finish by returning exactly the required container / item pairs whose accumulated quantities are greater than 500.
